!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/rpc.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/problem.tn"
@doc:"""
  The wire form of a protocol: one call of an exchange and its return. A packet is DATA, so this is a user
  schema, not part of the meta layer -- the meta layer says what a protocol IS; this says what calling one
  of its exchanges looks like on a wire.

  Both templates are CLOSED PER EXCHANGE by a wire schema derived from the protocol -- one entry per exchange,
  `place_order_call => call<new_order>`, `place_order_return => return<order, place_order_errors>` -- and a
  packet names the closed type: `!place_order_call { ... }`. That is what makes a packet fully typed: its
  request and response are checked against the protocol's own types by the resolver, an error arm is one
  of the exchange's declared errors with its status fixed, and this schema imports no service's types. The
  wire schema is deterministic in the protocol, so it is generated, published at its own identity, and
  fetched like any other; orders-wire-1.tn beside this file is one written by hand to show the shape.

  Both gateways translate INTO this. An http gateway turns an HTTP request into a call by the binding's
  placement run in reverse; an agent processor executes a compiled agent (agent-vm.tn), each of whose
  CALL instructions is one of these. In-process Java dispatch is the first transport; a remote one is one
  more. Several calls in one request, with data flowing between them, is the agent's business: a plan
  (agent.tn) is the AST that compiles to it.

  The exchange's address is a content-addressed identity: the protocol schema's `!!id` and the exchange's key,
  unambiguous across every service a gateway fronts, versioned by the identity, with no registry and no
  numeric ids -- and redundant with the closed type's name, kept because a packet should say where it is
  going without anyone parsing a type name.

  Precedents, and what each contributed: gRPC's closed set of transport-level statuses, its deadline, and
  its frames-plus-end-status model for a stream; JSON-RPC 2.0's self-contained envelope and correlation
  id; Avro's per-message declared errors, returned as typed values rather than codes. (Cap'n Proto's
  promised answers -- a call naming an earlier call's result by id and a path into it -- are the agent's
  precedent, in agent.tn's selectors.)
  """
!schema {
  @doc:"""
    One call of an exchange. `interface` and `method` are its address, the protocol schema and the
    exchange's key; `id` correlates a return with its call
    where a transport multiplexes, and is redundant over unary HTTP; `deadline` is the one piece of
    transport metadata a callee must know about, since it decides whether to start; `meta` is everything
    else -- tracing, credentials -- which this schema deliberately does not describe.

    `Req` is the exchange's request record. An exchange that takes nothing closes it with `void`.
    """
  call => <Req> {
    interface: uri
    method:    text
    id?:       text
    deadline?: duration
    meta?:     {text => text}
    request?:  Req
  }

  @doc:"""
    One outcome, and exactly one: the exchange's `response`, one of the `error`s its signature declared, or
    a `fault`. `Resp` is the response type; `Err` is the declared error type, or a choice of them, declared
    as an entry of its own, when the exchange declares several -- a choice of records, so the value carries
    the error's tag, which is the discriminator a caller wants. An error is a typed value composing
    `problem.details`, its status pinned on the type, and the resolver enforces the pin. A `fault` is the
    transport's or the processor's own failure, a `problem.details` whose `type` comes from the same small closed
    set the HTTP problem types use, so an http gateway maps by table rather than by translation. The group
    is what keeps business errors and faults apart, which is the distinction gRPC's single status channel
    blurs and the one a caller deciding whether to retry needs.
    """
  return => <Resp, Err> {
    id?: text
    ( response: Resp | error: Err | fault: problem.details )
  }

  @doc:"The return of an exchange that declares no errors: a response, or a fault."
  return_plain => <Resp> {
    id?: text
    ( response: Resp | fault: problem.details )
  }
}
